# Copy to .env and adjust. All variables have working defaults in docker-compose.yml.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Owner stream key - enforced at rtmp-ingest (the only public RTMP port). This
# is the real, security-relevant secret: rotate it before any deployment
# reachable from the internet. The SRT owner route (the srt-gateway-owner
# service, which scripts/setup.sh writes into docker-compose.override.yml)
# refuses to start without it, and with SRT_DIRECT=0 it is what authenticates
# that route's relay into /owner; on the default direct route (SRT_DIRECT=1)
# the gateway hands the stream to earshot and never presents this key. The
# SRT caller's own gate is SRT_OWNER_PASSPHRASE either way, not this key.
# Publish either of:
#   rtmp://<host>:1935/owner/<any-name>?token=<RTMP_OWNER_KEY>   <- PREFER THIS
#   rtmp://<host>:1935/owner/<RTMP_OWNER_KEY>                     <- works, but
#     puts the key in the stream NAME, which is relayed to earshot (logged at
#     info as publish: name='<key>'), interpolated into an ffmpeg argv visible
#     in the host process table, and forwarded to telemetry, which prints it.
#     The ?token= form does none of that: nginx strips it at /auth.
# OBS ME: Server rtmp://<host>:1935/owner?token=<RTMP_OWNER_KEY>  /  Stream Key: anything
#
# The value below is a PLACEHOLDER, not a usable secret: it is committed to a
# public repository, so treating it as one means every reader of this file can
# publish to your box. You do not have to pick one by hand: scripts/setup.sh
# generates a real 192-bit key here on first run, and replaces this placeholder
# if it is still in place.
#
# rtmp-ingest REFUSES TO START while this is still a placeholder, rather than
# coming up reachable on :1935 with a key the whole internet can read. That is
# deliberate and is the only credential treated this way. If you genuinely want
# it - an offline demo, a laptop on no network - set ALLOW_DEFAULT_OWNER_KEY=1
# and it will start with a warning on every boot instead.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
RTMP_OWNER_KEY=CHANGE_ME_this_default_is_public
#ALLOW_DEFAULT_OWNER_KEY=1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# loop-source's own token. scripts/setup.sh generates one, and rtmp-ingest
# REFUSES TO START on the committed default, for the same reason RTMP_OWNER_KEY
# does.
#
# This block used to say the opposite - "never reaches the public internet,
# safe to leave at the default" - and that was wrong in a way worth recording,
# because the reasoning sounded right. loop-source does only publish over the
# docker-internal network, but that describes who USES the token, not who may.
# nginx checks it inside the `owner` application, and :1935 is published on all
# interfaces, so the token was a valid owner-publish credential from anywhere -
# and it was a constant printed in a public repository. Reproduced 2026-08-10:
# an ffmpeg push to rtmp://<host>:1935/owner/<any-name>?token=hoast_demo was
# admitted. An owner publish preempts any live guest, stops the demo loop and
# becomes the stream.
#
# It is now scoped as well as secret: the token is only accepted together with
# the loop's own stream name (DASH_NAME), so it grants exactly the one publish
# loop-source makes and nothing else. Keeping it separate from RTMP_OWNER_KEY
# still makes sense; believing the separation was itself a boundary did not.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
LOOP_SOURCE_KEY=hoast_demo

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Public DASH manifest filename, served at /dash/<DASH_NAME>.mpd. Deliberately
# SEPARATE from RTMP_OWNER_KEY/LOOP_SOURCE_KEY: the publish name used to name
# this file, and any client on the public :1935 port chooses the publish
# name, so an interpolated name was an arbitrary-path write inside earshot.
# It is now fixed here and validated against [A-Za-z0-9_-]+ at earshot start.
# Must match the name the player page requests
# (services/hoast-player/index.html). Left at the default, either key can be
# rotated into a real secret without moving the manifest URL.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
DASH_NAME=hoast_demo

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Video codec policy at earshot's transcode (audio is ALWAYS 16-ch Opus,
# mapping_family 255 - hardcoded in earshot; do not downmix).
#
# The DEFAULT lives in docker-compose.yml's FFMPEG_FLAGS fallback and is
# deliberately not duplicated here, so the two can never disagree. To see what
# your host will actually run:
#
#     docker compose config | grep FFMPEG_FLAGS
#
# Both lines below are OVERRIDES: uncomment one to pin FFMPEG_FLAGS here rather
# than inherit the compose fallback. Note the second is byte-identical to that
# fallback today, so uncommenting it changes nothing now and only pins you
# against a future change of the default.
# The first is VP9 realtime (all-WebM segments) - the codec policy, but costly:
# it has been measured TWICE on the 2012 Mac Mini (quad-core i7) deployment host
# and both runs were over budget - ~310 % CPU scaled down to 1920x960, and
# 459.6 % CPU BELOW REALTIME at the unscaled 4K this line would actually run, so
# 4K VP9 here is measured-and-failed rather than untried. Figures, temperatures
# and the conditions that produced them are in the measurement notes (README,
# "Video codec"). The second is H.264 passthrough.
# NOTE: 2 s segments / -g 60 assume a 29.97/30 fps source (the demo content);
# use -g 50 for 25 fps. The rule is that segment duration must be an integer
# multiple of GOP duration: the muxer closes a segment at the first keyframe at
# or after the target, so -g 50 at 29.97 fps (a 1.668 s GOP) gives 3.34 s
# segments, i.e. two GOPs. Sub-multiple GOPs are valid but cost extra keyframes,
# so keep GOP equal to the segment duration for the live encode.
#
# suggestedPresentationDelay (>= 30 s, keeps players safely behind the live
# edge) is NOT settable by any ffmpeg flag - it is patched into the DASH muxer
# in services/earshot/src/Dockerfile (DASH_SPD_FLOOR build arg).
#
# VP9 must emit limited/tv colour range. The H.264 contribution is full-range
# (pc); full-range VP9 decodes fine as a plain file but breaks the dash.js/MSE
# player with PIPELINE_ERROR_DECODE on real GPUs, so convert it with the scale
# filter. The -c:v copy line below needs no conversion (it never re-encodes).
#FFMPEG_FLAGS=-window_size 100 -extra_window_size 30 -max_muxing_queue_size 1024 -vf scale=in_range=pc:out_range=tv -color_range tv -c:v libvpx-vp9 -deadline realtime -cpu-used 8 -row-mt 1 -b:v 4M -g 60 -keyint_min 60 -seg_duration 2 -dash_segment_type webm
#
# H.264 passthrough. This is what the compose fallback currently ships, and it
# is the cheap path (a Raspberry Pi, or debugging) - the trade the stack makes
# to stay within realtime on modest hardware. The DASH container follows the
# video codec and audio follows video, so -dash_segment_type mp4 puts BOTH
# tracks in fMP4 .m4s, audio still Opus (Opus-in-fMP4). Neither line mixes
# containers; all-WebM is what that same rule gives on the VP9 line above.
#
#FFMPEG_FLAGS=-window_size 100 -seg_duration 2 -max_muxing_queue_size 1000 -c:v copy -dash_segment_type mp4

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Delivery bitrate of the 16-channel Opus track.
#
# There is no separate variable for this. The rate is SET EXPLICITLY at 1024k in
# earshot's transcoder config ("-c:a libopus -mapping_family 255 -b:a 1024k",
# services/earshot/src/nginx-transcoder/nginx-no-ssl.conf:58, rationale in the
# comment beside it). FFMPEG_FLAGS is interpolated after that flag, so a later
# -b:a on whichever FFMPEG_FLAGS line above you enable wins, e.g. "... -c:v copy
# -b:a 512k".
#
# 1024k is not a tuning result: libopus targets 64 kb/s per uncoupled stream, so
# 16 discrete channels come to exactly this, and the flag only states the rate it
# would pick anyway. Cite it as a default made explicit. Lowering it is
# plausible (HOA channels above degree 0 carry far less energy than W) but
# untested; judge any change binaurally rendered, not on flat channels.
# Channel count and mapping_family 255 stay fixed.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Demo content: ./content/demo.mp4  (gitignored; loop-source idles until present)
#
# DEMO_CONTENT (default 1) automates demo provisioning at loop-source start:
#   - content/demo.mp4 missing -> synthesise a spherical placeholder
#     IN-CONTAINER (black sphere, test pattern as a front screen, one 440 Hz
#     source orbiting the listener in 3rd-order Ambisonics). No network.
#   - the two VOD reference masters missing -> fetch from the PINNED
#     vod-clips GitHub release (~185 MB once, background, SHA-256 verified
#     against hashes pinned in services/loop-source/fetch-demo-content.sh,
#     fail-soft: network trouble only costs the /vod/ masters, never the loop).
# Set 0 to do neither; the loop then idles until you provide demo.mp4.
# scripts/make-demo-loop.sh synthesises the same placeholder from the host.
#DEMO_CONTENT=0
#
# The file must be H.264 + 16-channel AAC with PCE headers (the RTMP leg
# cannot carry VP9/Opus, and loop-source streams it with `-c copy`).
# Preparing one from any 16-ch master, and the GOP rule the pipeline check
# enforces, is docs/DEMO-CONTENT.md.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# On-demand VOD (the /vod/ page and packaged reference clips; see README)
#
# OFF by default: the stack's purpose is live streaming, VOD is an addition.
# Disabled, no VOD route is served and the reference-master fetch is
# suppressed even with DEMO_CONTENT=1. The fetch needs BOTH flags on: it is
# auto-provisioning, so DEMO_CONTENT=0 suppresses it as well, per the
# `set 0 to do neither` above. VOD_ENABLED alone governs whether the /vod/
# route is served at all.
#VOD_ENABLED=1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Guest test endpoint (rtmp://<host>:1935/guest/<any-name>, no key; see README)
#
# OFF by default: most deployments are a single private publisher and should
# not expose a keyless application. Enabling is one switch, read by
# rtmp-ingest and telemetry at container start.
#
# YOU DO NOT NEED THIS TO STREAM YOUR OWN CONTENT. Both OBS guides use the
# authenticated OWNER route (UDP 8891, set up by scripts/setup.sh), which works
# with this left at 0. Turn it on only to let OTHER people push to your box
# without a key. If you do: with it at 0 the arbiter refuses every guest caller
# and OBS reports only a generic "Couldn't open ... I/O error", which looks like
# a network fault and is not one. Think hard on a host with a public IP: this
# endpoint takes no key from anybody.
GUEST_ENABLED=0
#
# One publisher at a time; the demo loop pauses for the session and resumes
# per the normal on-demand rule afterwards. The knobs below are read by
# telemetry at container start.
#
# Reconnect window after a guest disconnects (seconds). The slot is held this
# long before the session ends; a pusher arriving inside it from the SAME
# source address continues the SAME session (names are not identities without
# keys, so the address is the only thing tying a reconnect to its session -
# anyone else waits the window out).
#GUEST_GRACE_S=120
#
# Absolute session cap (seconds). Enforced within one liveness-ping interval
# (~10 s) of expiry, so it is soft by up to that much. Reconnecting does not
# reset the clock.
#GUEST_MAX_S=10800
#
# Cooldown after a FORCED session end (cap expiry or the dashboard kill):
# guest publishes are refused this long, so an auto-reconnecting encoder
# cannot re-claim the slot instantly (300 s only spaces out OBS's retries;
# its default reconnect schedule keeps trying for hours). A natural stop has
# no cooldown. 0 disables.
#GUEST_COOLDOWN_S=300
#
# Resource guard. Without it a guest can push whatever bitrate they like at
# whatever hardware this runs on, and the only limits are the 3 h cap and your
# nerve. BOTH DEFAULT TO 0 (off) because the right values are a property of
# your host, not of this stack: measure them, do not copy them.
#
# GUEST_MAX_TEMP_C is the safety net and should be the number you trust. It is
# read from the host thermal zone, so it accounts for room temperature, dust,
# thermal soak and anything else a bitrate figure cannot see. Set it below the
# CPU's throttle point (a 2012 Mac Mini throttles near 100 C, so 95 works).
#
# GUEST_MAX_MBPS is a coarse pre-filter for the obvious abuser, not a safety
# net. Measured evidence for why the distinction matters: on that Mac Mini a
# synthetic 42 Mbps ladder peaked at 87 C, while a REAL 45.5 Mbps session ran
# 87-96 C with zero viewers - a ~9 C gap no bitrate threshold could predict.
# Reference points from the same ladder (4K30, video passthrough): 8 Mbps
# -> 72 C peak, 18 -> 76, 42 -> 87, 59 -> 93, and the encoder never fell below
# realtime, so this is a thermal limit rather than a capability one.
#
# Neither fires on a single sample: a limit must hold for GUEST_LIMIT_STRIKES
# consecutive liveness pings (~10 s apart) before the session ends, and one
# good sample forgives the strikes, so a brief spike or one hot read does not
# end a broadcast. The pusher is told which limit they hit on the player page.
#GUEST_MAX_TEMP_C=95
#GUEST_MAX_MBPS=20
#GUEST_LIMIT_STRIKES=3

# --- SRT guest ingest (srt-gateway service; needs GUEST_ENABLED=1 to admit) ---
#
# Native OBS multitrack over SRT, and the RECOMMENDED contribution path: one
# recipe that works with stock OBS on both macOS and Windows, no patched fork
# and no separate merge step. Guests use Custom Output (FFmpeg) - Container
# mpegts, plain aac encoder ("Show all codecs"; never mp2 or a CoreAudio/AT
# variant), 4 audio tracks of 4 channels - pushed to
#   srt://<host>:8890?streamid=<display-name>&latency=2000000&pkt_size=1128
# (latency is in MICROSECONDS in OBS's URL; 2000000 = 2 s). The gateway joins
# the four tracks to one 16-ch stream and republishes into the same guest
# arbiter as RTMP, so slot/cap/cooldown/grace/bans behave identically on both
# transports.
#
# ON by default. The port binds, but the arbiter still refuses every caller
# unless GUEST_ENABLED=1, so this alone does not open the endpoint to anyone.
# Set 0 to leave the UDP port unbound entirely.
SRT_ENABLED=1
#
# OPTIONAL. srt-gateway tells telemetry each caller's real IP so bans and
# session logs key the guest rather than the gateway's own container address.
# That claim is already authenticated by the gateway's address - nginx-rtmp
# reports the publisher's address itself and a remote publisher cannot forge
# it - and setting a secret here (openssl rand -hex 16) additionally requires
# a matching token. Belt and braces, not a prerequisite.
#GUEST_GW_SECRET=
#
# Host-side UDP port (the container side is fixed at 8890).
#SRT_PORT=8890
#
# SRT receive latency in ms (the listener's reorder/retransmit budget; the
# effective value is the max of both ends, so OBS's 2000000 us matches this).
#SRT_LATENCY_MS=2000
#
# Optional HaiCrypt pre-shared passphrase (10-79 chars). Published openly in
# the guest doc it stays keyless-in-spirit while making the port useless to
# random scanners; also the knob to reach for if a network admin wants the
# endpoint encrypted.
#
# THIS IS THE GUEST ONE, and it is not the passphrase the OBS guides talk about.
# Yours for pushing your own stream is SRT_OWNER_PASSPHRASE, further down, which
# scripts/setup.sh generates. Two separate secrets with OPPOSITE disclosure
# policies: this one is meant to be handed out, that one gates owner publish and
# must never be. Do not set them to the same value.
#
# Each gateway reads its own name, in .env and inside its container alike, so
# `docker compose exec srt-gateway-owner printenv SRT_OWNER_PASSPHRASE` says
# what you expect. Before 2026-08-10 both containers read a shared
# SRT_PASSPHRASE and this file had to use a different name purely so the roles
# could not collide; the separation is structural now rather than a convention
# to remember. If you have SRT_PASSPHRASE set from before, the gateway refuses
# to start rather than dropping it silently, and setup.sh renames it for you.
#SRT_GUEST_PASSPHRASE=
#
# --- SRT OWNER route: direct-to-DASH (owner only; default ON) ---
#
# The owner's SRT stream is handed to earshot's own transcoder as a bare
# mpegts remux, instead of being re-encoded to 16-channel AAC and
# republished over RTMP/FLV. FLV cannot carry 16 discrete channels without
# a named layout, so the legacy hop paid for a full AAC decode/join/encode
# on every frame; the direct hop deletes it. Measured on a 2012 Mac Mini at
# 20 Mbps: ~28-42 % of a core against the legacy ~90-130 %, and one lossy
# audio generation removed (OBS AAC -> Opus, no AAC in between).
#
# scripts/setup.sh writes the line `SRT_DIRECT=${SRT_DIRECT:-1}` VERBATIM into
# the generated docker-compose.override.yml (its heredoc is quoted), so Compose
# resolves it from this file at every `up` - the same way RTMP_OWNER_KEY works.
# Changing it here and recreating srt-gateway-owner is therefore enough on any
# install whose override carries that line. An override written before
# 2026-08-09, or hand-assembled from an older example, may hardcode the value
# instead: check it before assuming this knob does anything.
#
# Set to 0 to keep the legacy RTMP republish. Reasons you might: you want the
# owner's stream to appear in rtmp-ingest's stat page and fail2ban log the way
# guests do, or you are bisecting a problem across the two routes.
#
# This knob is the OWNER route only. Guests have their own, below.
#SRT_DIRECT=1
#
# The same direct route for GUESTS, on by default like the owner's since
# 2026-08-21, the day a stranger's real session first ran it end to end -
# admitted by the arbiter, all sixteen channels in order. (It had been carried
# end to end by the operators since 2026-08-10; the shipped default waited for
# the untrusted case.)
#
# Turning it on does NOT weaken guest admission. Auth, the single slot, the kick
# lever, bans and the session cap hang off telemetry's session protocol
# (/gw/session/claim|beat|done, peer-address + secret), which the direct route
# speaks too; earshot's listener refuses any connection that is not a gateway.
# What it drops is the 16-channel AAC re-encode and its CPU, the same saving the
# owner route already takes.
#
# Two prerequisites, and it fails differently for each: without GUEST_GW_SECRET
# the session routes fail closed, and without SRT_DIRECT_LISTENERS=1 the claim
# succeeds and the dial then hits a port nothing is listening on.
# Set 0 to fall back to the legacy RTMP republish for guests. Reasons you
# might: the republish kill path is nginx refusing the guest's next ping
# (older and blunter than the direct route's session kick), the publisher
# then shows in rtmp-ingest's stat page, and earshot only ever receives
# gateway-re-encoded AAC rather than the sender's own bytes.
#GUEST_SRT_DIRECT=0
#
# Arms earshot's four compose-internal listeners (:9100 to :9103, one per audio
# shape and video codec: 4x4 or 1x4, H.264 or H.265) that
# the direct route feeds. Leave on; SRT_DIRECT=1 with these disabled dials a
# port nothing is listening on.
#SRT_DIRECT_LISTENERS=1

# How long guest session/report logs keep source IPs before the redaction
# job blanks them (rows stay forever as anonymised statistics), and how long
# a dashboard ban lasts. GUEST_BAN_DAYS is clamped to GUEST_RETENTION_DAYS:
# ban expiry and IP redaction are the same event by design, since a ban
# whose address has been anonymised cannot match anything. IP bans are a
# speed bump, not enforcement (dynamic IPs, CGNAT and VPNs defeat them).
#GUEST_RETENTION_DAYS=30
#GUEST_BAN_DAYS=30

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional reachability probes on the :8090 dashboard (no credentials).
# TUNNEL_METRICS_URL: cloudflared's local metrics server; the tunnel dropping
# is the one failure where the host looks healthy while unreachable from
# outside. Needs the telemetry container to reach the host (extra_hosts:
# host.docker.internal:host-gateway in an override).
# VOD_PROBE_URL: HEAD on one known object of an external VOD origin (e.g. the
# R2 custom domain); if it fails, remove vodBase from brand.json to fall back.
#TUNNEL_METRICS_URL=http://host.docker.internal:20241
#VOD_PROBE_URL=https://vod.example.com/vod-dash/clip/clip.mpd

# Optional Cloudflare Web Analytics on the :8090 dashboard: VOD page loads
# (arrivals, not playback) via the GraphQL Analytics API. Token needs only
# Account Analytics Read. Pairs with cfBeaconToken in brand.json. Both empty
# = feature off, a deployer without Cloudflare sees no difference.
#CF_ANALYTICS_TOKEN=
#CF_ACCOUNT_ID=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ffmpeg licence stamp (services/earshot/README.md section 7, upstream PR #57)
#
# The stack builds earshot WITHOUT --enable-nonfree by default: nothing in
# this build needs it, and without it the image is redistributable (plain
# GPLv3 via libx264). Set 1 to restore the stock upstream configure line.
#ENABLE_NONFREE=1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Host address the player (:8080) binds to. Default 127.0.0.1 keeps it on
# loopback: reachable through the cloudflared tunnel and local reverse proxies
# (which connect on localhost) but never directly on the LAN. Set 0.0.0.0 to
# expose it on all interfaces for direct LAN access on a trusted network. To
# reach it over Tailscale as well, leave this at the default and add a second
# bind to the box's Tailscale IP in docker-compose.override.yml, mirroring how
# the :8090/:8081 panels are bound (ports lists are merged, not replaced).
#PLAYER_BIND=127.0.0.1
